TERM SHEET SUNDAY
Sunday, March 8, 2026

The week in venture, for founders who read the documents. Clocks moved overnight; this is still 8am.

THE PRO-RATA SQUEEZE
In hot rounds, lead investors are increasingly asking existing investors to waive or reduce pro-rata to make room. In February we counted eleven Series A and B rounds where seed investors were cut back below their contractual right. Founders are caught between a lead who wants ownership and a seed investor who has a signed right. The playbook that works: raise the round size slightly, let the seed investors take a partial pro-rata, and document the waiver formally. Informal waivers come back as grievances.

SECONDARIES AT SEED
Founders selling a small slice of their own stock at the Series A is no longer unusual. In our sample, 18% of Series A rounds over $15M included a founder secondary, median 3% of the founder's holding. Investors have mostly stopped objecting, on the theory that a founder with a paid-off mortgage takes better long-term risks. Two cautions: the secondary is usually at a discount to the round price, and the tax treatment depends on holding period, so talk to an accountant before the term sheet, not after.

WHAT "CLEAN" MEANS IN 2026
A clean Series A term sheet today: 1x non-participating preference, broad-based weighted-average anti-dilution, a board of two founders, one investor and one independent, standard protective provisions, and an employee equity refresh sized to an actual hiring plan rather than a round number. Anything beyond that is a negotiation, and you should know why the other side wants it.

ROUNDS THIS WEEK
- Two vertical AI companies in logistics raised Series Bs above $50M, both from specialist leads.
- A well-known seed fund announced a $400M fund IV, larger than its first three combined.
- An industrial-software company we like quietly extended its seed rather than raise an A. Smart, in this market.

See you next Sunday.

